  "worked_solution": "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\n{R}_{\\theta }=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta $ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta $ are positive we must have that $\\theta $ lies in $1$st quadrant. \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nFinally, to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14098280/assets/87620581d28a4a3fd832785947ec6fc043d51841bcedd414b264121d7bf1c458.png)",
